('f30ddded-f441-55ed-8dec-bf0aa5771b2d', 'ec015167-65fb-47df-b565-1055132ab4f3', 'c16862d4-9430-4015-95fa-20d7cf89a45d', 'a-level', '2.7', '9702 coursebook 2.7 · Determining velocity and acceleration in the laboratory', 45, 45, 'In an experiment s is measured for several t from rest: (0.50 s, 0.15 m), (1.00 s, 0.60 m), (1.50 s, 1.35 m), (2.00 s, 2.40 m). (a) Explain why s should be plotted against t². (b) Calculate the gradient using the first and last points. (c) Calculate the acceleration.', NULL, 3, 'numeric'::answer_type, '1.2', 'm s⁻²', false, NULL, false, NULL, '(a) s = ½at², so s against t² is a straight line through the origin of gradient a/2. (b) Gradient = (2.40 − 0.15) ÷ (2.00² − 0.50²) = 0.60 m s⁻². (c) a = 2 × 0.60 = 1.2 m s⁻². Mark scheme (6 marks). B1: linear form. C1: t² values. C1: gradient. M1: a = 2 × gradient. C1: value. A1: a = 1.2 m s⁻².', 6, '<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 400 290" role="img" aria-labelledby="fg124af813-t fg124af813-d" style="font-family:ui-sans-serif,system-ui,-apple-system,''Segoe UI'',Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;max-width:100%;height:auto"><title id="fg124af813-t">s against t²</title><desc id="fg124af813-d">Points for s against t²: (0.25, 0.15), (1.00, 0.60), (2.25, 1.35) and (4.00, 2.40), on a straight line through the origin.</desc><line x1="33" y1="34" x2="33" y2="242" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1d5db);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="120.3" y1="34" x2="120.3" y2="242" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1d5db);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="207.5" y1="34" x2="207.5" y2="242" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1d5db);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="294.8" y1="34" x2="294.8" y2="242" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1d5db);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="382" y1="34" x2="382" y2="242" style="stroke:var(--