('1e652bb5-16c6-5910-8af1-c5eda253e150', 'ec015167-65fb-47df-b565-1055132ab4f3', '69f49b38-4492-521c-be78-56effb528ad2', 'a-level', '2.8', '9702 coursebook 2.8 · The equations of motion', 52, 52, 'A parachutist falls freely from rest for 3.0 s and then opens the parachute, decelerating at 12 m s⁻² until her speed is 5.0 m s⁻¹. (a) Calculate the speed when the parachute opens. (b) Calculate the distance fallen while decelerating. Take g = 9.81 m s⁻².', NULL, 3, 'numeric'::answer_type, '35.05', 'm', false, NULL, false, NULL, '(a) v = 9.81 × 3.0 = 29.43 m s⁻¹. (b) s = (29.43² − 5.0²) ÷ (2 × 12) = 35.0 m. Mark scheme (5 marks). C1: free-fall speed. M1: v² = u² + 2as. C1: substitution. C1: value. A1: s = 35.0 m.', 5, '<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 400 290" role="img" aria-labelledby="fg181d1fba-t fg181d1fba-d" style="font-family:ui-sans-serif,system-ui,-apple-system,''Segoe UI'',Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;max-width:100%;height:auto"><title id="fg181d1fba-t">Parachute opening</title><desc id="fg181d1fba-d">A v–t graph rising at 9.81 m s⁻² for 3.0 s, falling at 12 m s⁻² to 5.0 m s⁻¹, then constant (down positive).</desc><line x1="29.4" y1="34" x2="29.4" y2="242" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1d5db);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="88.2" y1="34" x2="88.2" y2="242" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1d5db);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="147" y1="34" x2="147" y2="242" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1d5db);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="205.7" y1="34" x2="205.7" y2="242" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1d5db);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="264.5" y1="34" x2="264.5" y2="242" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1d5db);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="323.2" y1="34" x2="323.2" y2="242" style="stroke:var(--fig-gri