troke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><text x="32.8" y="117.2" font-size="12" text-anchor="end" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">0.5</text><line x1="35.8" y1="75.6" x2="39.8" y2="75.6" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><text x="32.8" y="75.6" font-size="12" text-anchor="end" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">1.0</text><line x1="35.8" y1="34" x2="39.8" y2="34" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><text x="32.8" y="34" font-size="12" text-anchor="end" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">1.5</text><text x="210.9" y="274" font-size="14" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">t / s</text><text x="35.8" y="14" font-size="14" text-anchor="start" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">a / m s⁻²</text><polyline points="39.8,59 108.2,59 108.2,158.8 279.3,158.8 279.3,225.4 382,225.4" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent,#1d4ed8);stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/></svg>', 'An a–t graph: +1.2 m s⁻² for 2.0 s, zero for 5.0 s, then −0.80 m s⁻² for 3.0 s.'),
('740ff135-3cd8-5fa4-b236-b1e72095503b', 'ec015167-65fb-47df-b565-1055132ab4f3', '643b6cd1-3fbe-4618-9330-b9d737f06161', 'a-level', '2.10', '9702 coursebook 2.10 · Uniform and non-uniform acceleration', 47, 47, 'A sphere in oil reaches terminal velocity 0.25 m s⁻¹. Its speed is 0.20 m s⁻¹ at t = 0.10 s and 0.24 m s⁻¹ at t = 0.20 s. (a) Calculate its average acceleration in this interval. (b) State and explain its acceleration at terminal velocity.', NULL, 3, 'numeric'::answer_type, '0.4', 'm s⁻²', false, NULL, false, NULL, '(a) a = (0.24 − 0.20) ÷ 0.10 = 0.40 m s⁻². (b) Zero: the weight is balanced by drag and upthrust, so the velocity is constant. Mark scheme (5 marks). C1: Δv. C1: value. B1: zero. B1: balanced forces. A1: a = 0.40 m s⁻².', 5, NULL, NULL),
('4527597c-10dc-5263-bf50-c633d937cf17', 'ec015167-65fb-47df-b565-1055132ab4f3', '643b6cd1-3fbe-4618-9330-b9d737f06161', 'a-level', '2.10', '9702 coursebook 2.10 · Uniform and non-uniform acceleration', 48, 48, 'A falling ball''s speed is recorded as 0, 9.0, 16.5, 22.0, 25.5 m s⁻¹ at 0, 1, 2, 3, 4 s. (a) Show the acceleration is non-uniform. (b) Estimate the distance fallen in 4 s using trapezia. (c) Compare with the distance if a were 9.81 m s⁻² throughout. Take g = 9.81 m s⁻².', NULL, 3, 'numeric'::answer_type, '18.23', 'm', false, NULL, false, NULL, '(a) Δv per second: 9.0, 7.5, 5.5, 3.5, not constant. (b) s = 4.5 + 12.75 + 19.25 + 23.75 = 60.25 m. (c) 0.5 × 9.81 × 4² = 78.48 m, so 78.48 − 60.25 = 18.2 m more. Mark scheme (6 marks). C1: changing Δv. M1: trapezium areas. C1: estimate. C1: free-fall value. C1: difference. A1: Δs = 18.2 m.', 6, '<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 400 290" role="img" aria-labelledby="fgae706027-t fgae706027-d" style="font-family:ui-sans-serif,system-ui,-apple-system,''Segoe UI'',Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;max-width:100%;height:auto"><title id="fgae706027-t">Falling with air resistance</title><desc id="fgae706027-d">Speeds 0, 9.0, 16.5, 22.0 and 25.5 m s⁻¹ at 0–4 s (joined), with the free-fall line v = 9.81t dashed for comparison.</desc><line x1="29.4" y1="34" x2="29.4" y2="242" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1d5db);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="117.6" y1="34" x2="117.6" y2="242" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1d5db);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="205.7" y1="34" x2="205.7" y2="242" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1d5db);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="293.9" y1="34" x2="293.9" y2="242" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1d5db);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="382" y1="34" x2="382" y2="242" style="stroke:var(--fig-gri